WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.949
Conditioning restricts attention to the outcomes where the given event happened

00:00:04.949 --> 00:00:10.324
Among eight people, six play guitar, four play piano, and two play both

00:00:10.324 --> 00:00:15.700
Knowing someone plays guitar changes the comparison group from eight people to six.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:22.329
Within the guitar group, only the two people in the overlap play piano

00:00:22.329 --> 00:00:26.018
Therefore the conditional probability is two divided by six

00:00:26.018 --> 00:00:32.167
The numerator describes both events, while the denominator describes the event we are already assuming.

00:00:33.467 --> 00:00:35.969
Reversing the condition changes the denominator

00:00:35.969 --> 00:00:42.643
Among the four piano players, two also play guitar, giving one half instead of one third

00:00:42.643 --> 00:00:48.900
Conditional probabilities with the same two events are generally different when their roles are reversed.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:55.031
Independence means learning one event does not change the other's probability

00:00:55.031 --> 00:01:02.057
Here the observed overlap is one quarter, while multiplying the two unconditional probabilities gives three eighths

00:01:02.057 --> 00:01:07.767
They differ, so guitar and piano membership are not independent in this example.
